# Hardware Tests

This directory contains hardware and communication tests for the Arduino Due firmware.

These tests are development and diagnostic tools. They are not part of the main runtime architecture of the product.

## Test Structure

```text
test/
├── spi/
│   └── spi_test.cpp
│
├── cc1101/
│   └── cc1101_test.cpp
│
└── ethernet/
    ├── ethernet_detect_test.cpp
    ├── ethernet_register_test.cpp
    ├── ethernet_network_test.cpp
    └── ethernet_tcp_test.cpp
```

## SPI Test

`spi_test.cpp`

Tests the basic SPI communication of the Arduino Due.

The purpose is to verify that the Due SPI controller and physical SPI connection are working correctly.

This test does not depend on the CC1101 or Ethernet Shield.

## CC1101 Test

`cc1101_test.cpp`

Tests communication between the Arduino Due and the CC1101 module.

The test is used to diagnose SPI communication and CC1101 connectivity before implementing the actual radio functionality.

## Ethernet Detect Test

`ethernet_detect_test.cpp`

Checks whether the Ethernet Shield is detected by the Ethernet library.

This test verifies the presence of the W5100 Ethernet controller.

## Ethernet Register Test

`ethernet_register_test.cpp`

Tests low-level read/write access to W5100 registers.

This is a hardware-level diagnostic test and is useful when Ethernet initialization fails.

## Ethernet Network Test

`ethernet_network_test.cpp`

Initializes the W5100 with the configured network settings and verifies the local network configuration.

It checks values such as:

* Ethernet hardware status
* Local IP address
* Subnet mask
* Gateway

This test verifies the Ethernet network layer before testing TCP.

## Ethernet TCP Test

`ethernet_tcp_test.cpp`

Tests the TCP server functionality of the Arduino Due.

The Due opens a TCP listener on the configured port and waits for incoming TCP connections.

The test verifies:

```text
Ethernet
    ↓
TCP Listener
    ↓
Incoming TCP Connection
    ↓
Receive Data
```

This test does not implement the project's HTTP protocol.

Its purpose is only to verify that the Due can:

1. Start a TCP listener.
2. Accept an incoming TCP connection.
3. Receive bytes from the client.
4. Optionally send bytes back to the client.

After TCP communication is confirmed, the main project can build the HTTP server on top of the same TCP infrastructure.

## Test Selection

Tests are selected through `TestConfigs::ActiveTest`.

For example:

```cpp
namespace TestConfigs
{
    constexpr TestMode ActiveTest = TestMode::ETHERNET_TCP;
}
```

Only one diagnostic test should be active at a time.

When `ActiveTest` is `TestMode::NONE`, the normal firmware starts.

## Important Architecture Rule

The production architecture uses the Arduino Due as an HTTP server.

The Due does not connect to Flask as a TCP client.

The intended communication flow is:

```text
Flask / Other Client
        │
        │ HTTP over TCP
        ▼
Arduino Due
        │
        │ HTTP Server
        ▼
HTTP Parser
        │
        ▼
Command Router
        │
        ▼
Hardware
```

Therefore, `ethernet_tcp_test.cpp` is only a diagnostic test of the lower-level TCP layer.

It must not introduce a runtime dependency where the Due connects to Flask.

## Test Layering

The tests are intentionally separated:

```text
SPI
 ↓
CC1101 / W5100
 ↓
Ethernet
 ↓
TCP
 ↓
HTTP
 ↓
Application Protocol
```

Each test should verify one layer before the next layer is implemented.
